this Canadian microbrewery only makes  pure rice wine called June my shoe the  rice is milled before it arrives at the  brewery milling removes the proteins and  lipids on the outside layers of the rice  grain first they wash the rice then they  pour it in a porous bag and soak it in  cold filtered water once it reaches the  right consistency they drain the bag  overnight then they pour the rice into a  steamer the steamer cooks the rice under  pressure for about an hour they cover  the steamer with cloth to contain the  rice as it cooks the steamer softens the  rice but doesn't overcook it to ferment  at the right speed the rice needs to be  firm on the outside and soft in the  center workers separate the rice by hand  then use fans to cool the rice down to  about 98 degrees next they transfer it  to a heated room they spread out the  rice and cover it with mold spores  imported from Japan  when heated the spores germinate and  grow on the rice the spores secrete  enzymes that will transform the starch  into fermentable sugars workers insert a  thermometer into the rice then wrap it  in cloth to hold in the heat 48 hours  later the sweet rice called Koji is  ready now they begin the fermentation  process they mix three quarters of  steamed rice with one quarter of Koji in  a temperature controlled fermentation  tank then they add spring water in yeast  the mold enzymes in the coachee convert  the starch in the steamed rice into  sugars the yeast consumes those sugars  and produces alcohol the process takes  about three weeks the brew master checks  the temperature acidity residual sugar  and alcohol levels every day when the  brew is ready they pour it into cloth  bags they neatly stack the bags in a  traditional Japanese mechanical press as  the press fills up the weight of the  bags kick starts depressing they seal  the press with heavy steel plates and  now the pressing mechanism  as they turn the crank the press  squeezes all the liquid out of the backs  when they're done only unfermented rice  will be left behind the liquid saki  drains out of a spout at the base of the  press  they aged the sake and refrigerated  tanks for up to four months the sake  this brewery produces is unpasteurized  this enhances the flavor but requires  refrigeration  you
 

